WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.082
Before writing a pivot, complete one sentence: one output row represents what?

00:00:05.082 --> 00:00:10.247
Once the row meaning is clear, identify which category values should become columns.

00:00:10.247 --> 00:00:13.142
Then identify the measure that belongs in each cell.

00:00:13.142 --> 00:00:16.982
Finally ask whether several source rows can map to the same cell.

00:00:16.982 --> 00:00:19.887
If they can, you need an aggregation rule.

00:00:19.887 --> 00:00:24.292
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:24.292 --> 00:00:29.927
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:29.927 --> 00:00:31.857
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:31.857 --> 00:00:37.607
In a real setting such as a BI dashboard turning monthly categories into presentation columns,

00:00:37.607 --> 00:00:44.322
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:44.322 --> 00:00:44.762
before discussing syntax.

00:00:44.762 --> 00:00:50.480
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
